**The problem:** the CSS `transform: translate(-50%, -50%)` and the GSAP `x`/`y` tween both write to the same `transform` property. GSAP's start value fights the CSS value, so the heading loses its centering offset, and `lint` rejects it with `gsap_css_transform_conflict`.

**The fix:** take centering out of `transform` and let layout do it, then give GSAP sole ownership of the transform with explicit start and end values.

Before:

```html
<h1 id="title" style="position:absolute; top:50%; left:50%; transform:translate(-50%,-50%);">Heading</h1>
<script>
  tl.from("#title", { y: 40, opacity: 0, duration: 0.6 }, 0.2);
</script>
```

After (recommended: flex-centred wrapper, tween the child):

```html
<div class="title-wrap">
  <h1 id="title">Heading</h1>
</div>

<style>
  .title-wrap {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
  }
  #title {
    display: block;   /* transformed elements must be block-level + sized */
    margin: 0;
    opacity: 0;
  }
</style>

<script>
  const tl = gsap.timeline({ paused: true });
  tl.fromTo(
    "#title",
    { x: 0, y: 40, opacity: 0 },
    { x: 0, y: 0, opacity: 1, duration: 0.6, ease: "power3.out" },
    0.2
  );
  window.__timelines["<root data-composition-id>"] = tl;
</script>
```

- **Centering is preserved:** the flex wrapper centres the heading, so no transform is needed for position and GSAP's `x`/`y` are pure offsets from the centred spot.
- **The animation stays deterministic:** `fromTo` declares both endpoints, so seeking to any frame gives the same result regardless of CSS or the order frames are visited. It runs on the single paused timeline registered under the root's `data-composition-id`.

**Alternative if you must keep `top:50%; left:50%`:** delete the CSS `transform` and let GSAP own the percentage offset alongside the pixel motion:

```js
tl.fromTo(
  "#title",
  { xPercent: -50, yPercent: -50, y: 40, opacity: 0 },
  { xPercent: -50, yPercent: -50, y: 0, opacity: 1, duration: 0.6, ease: "power3.out" },
  0.2
);
```

`xPercent`/`yPercent` are separate from `x`/`y` in GSAP, so the two no longer collide.

If the heading is itself a `.clip`, tween an inner child rather than the clip element. Neither version has been run or linted here, since this was a text-only answer.

